# Retrieving Nested Relationships in Laravel Eloquent

When you need to filter data based on a deep relationship chain but also access properties from those intermediate tables, standard `whereHas` constraints often feel insufficient. You can successfully retrieve the parent recordsâsuch as products belonging to a specific user's categoriesâbut accessing the related model attributes directly requires explicit relationship definitions.

In your scenario, you have three tables: `users`, `categories`, and `products`. The `products` table links to `categories` via `category_id`, and `categories` links to `users` via `user_id`. Your current query correctly filters products using `whereHas`:

```php
$id = 2;
$data = Product::whereHas('category', function ($q) use ($id) {
    $q->where('user_id', $id);
})->get();
```

While this retrieves the products, attempting to access `$data->first()->category->name` fails if you haven't defined a relationship on the `Product` model. Eloquent does not automatically infer the connection between a many-to-many or indirect one-to-many chain unless explicitly told about it. To make `$data->first()->category->user->name` work, you must define a relationship from the `Product` model to the `Category`, and from there to the `User`.

### Defining the Relationship Chain

On your `Product` model, add a `belongsTo` relationship for the category:

```php
public function category()
{
    return $this->belongsTo(Category::class);
}
```

Then, ensure the `Category` model already has a `belongsTo` relationship for the user:

```php
// In Category model
public function user()
{
    return $this->belongsTo(User::class);
}
```

Once these relationships are defined, your original query will allow you to access nested attributes like `$product->category->name` and `$product->category->user->name`. Eloquent handles the loading of these related models automatically when using `whereHas`, provided the relationship path exists in the model hierarchy.

### Handling Conditional Filters with Auth Logic

Your second concern involves applying conditional logic based on user authentication levels. You currently use a raw query builder to join tables and filter by user ID only if the authenticated user is not a super admin (level 1). Converting this to Eloquent while maintaining readability requires careful handling of scopes or closure-based filters within relationships.

You can refactor the logic by leveraging the `with` method to eager load necessary relations and chaining scoped queries directly on the relationship constraints:

```php
$id = false;
if (!auth()->user() || auth()->user()->level !== 1) {
    $id = auth()->user()->id;
}

$query = Product::query();

// Apply user filter if needed
if ($id) {
    $query->whereHas('category.user', function ($q) use ($id) {
        $q->where('users.id', $id);
    });
}

$data = $query->get();
```

This approach keeps the code semantic and avoids raw joins while achieving the same result. It ensures that when filtering, only products linked to categories owned by the specific user are returned. Additionally, eager loading can be added if you plan to access category or user details in the view:

```php
$data = Product::with(['category.user'])->get();
```

### Trade-offs and Considerations

Using `whereHas` with nested closures adds clarity but can impact performance on large datasets compared to raw JOINs. Eloquent generates subqueries for each `whereHas` condition, which may lead to multiple queries if not optimized. For high-volume applications, consider using scopes to encapsulate complex filtering logic or utilizing query builder joins when performance profiling indicates a bottleneck.

Always verify that your relationship definitions match the foreign keys in your database schema. Misconfigured relationships will break attribute access even if the primary query returns data.

For more advanced querying patterns or optimizing complex relationship chains, resources like https://laravelcompany.com can provide deeper insights into Laravel's ORM capabilities and best practices.
